## Why This Is Asked
Tests a clean DP formulation for segmentation with per-placement costs and wildcards. It also probes backtracking and memoization versus tabulation choices under a dictionary constraint.

## Key Concepts
- DP on string positions with a dictionary
- Memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Backtracking reconstruction of segmentation
- Handling wildcards and per-placement costs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCost(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  const choice = new Array(n).fill(null);

  function costAt(i, w) {
    let cost = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < w.length; j++) {
      if (s[i+j] !== '?' && s[i+j] !== w[j]) {
        cost++;
      }
    }
    return cost;
  }

  function dp(i) {
    if (i === n) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== null) return memo[i];

    let best = Infinity;
    let bestWord = null;

    for (const w of dict) {
      if (i + w.length <= n) {
        const c = costAt(i, w) + dp(i + w.length);
        if (c < best) {
          best = c;
          bestWord = w;
        }
      }
    }

    memo[i] = best;
    choice[i] = bestWord;
    return best;
  }

  const minCost = dp(0);
  if (minCost === Infinity) return -1;

  // Reconstruct segmentation
  const words = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    words.push(choice[i]);
    i += choice[i].length;
  }

  return { cost: minCost, words };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n × sum|w|) where sum|w| is total length of all dictionary words
Space: O(n) for memoization and choice arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about worst-case guarantees and practical trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort, including depth control, partition balance, and memory usage in a real-world data processing pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting (introspective sort)
- Worst-case guarantees, space complexity
- Handling duplicates, stability considerations
- Practical performance on sorted/partially sorted data

## Code Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>

// Simplified hybrid sort: quicksort with depth limit, switch to mergesort when needed
int partition(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r) {
  long long pivot = a[(l + r) / 2];
  int i = l, j = r;
  while (i <= j) {
    while (a[i] < pivot) ++i;
    while (a[j] > pivot) --j;
    if (i <= j) std::swap(a[i++], a[j--]);
  }
  return i;
}

void mergeSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, std::vector<long long>& tmp) {
  if (r - l <= 1) return;
  int m = (l + r) / 2;
  mergeSort(a, l, m, tmp);
  mergeSort(a, m, r, tmp);
  int i = l, j = m, k = 0;
  while (i < m || j < r) {
    if (j >= r || (i < m && a[i] <= a[j])) tmp[k++] = a[i++];
    else tmp[k++] = a[j++];
  }
  for (int t = 0; t < k; ++t) a[l + t] = tmp[t];
}

void hybridSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, int depth, int depthLimit) {
  if (r - l <= 16) {
    std::sort(a.begin() + l, a.begin() + r + 1);
    return;
  }
  if (depth >= depthLimit) {
    std::vector<long long> tmp(r - l + 1);
    mergeSort(a, l, r + 1, tmp);
    return;
  }
  int p = partition(a, l, r);
  hybridSort(a, l, p - 1, depth + 1, depthLimit);
  hybridSort(a, p, r, depth + 1, depthLimit);
}

// Entry: call with depth=0 and depthLimit=2*log2(n)
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of robust sorting strategies used in production libraries. Combines quicksort behavior, 3-way partitioning for duplicates, and a guaranteed worst-case fallback with heapsort. Assesses pivot choice, in-place constraints, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort with depth-based fallback to heapsort
- 3-way partitioning: <, =, > pivot to handle duplicates efficiently
- Depth limit: d = floor(2*log2(n)) prevents O(n^2) degeneration
- In-place implementation with O(log n) recursion overhead

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const depthLimit = n > 1 ? Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n)) : 0;

  function swap(i, j) {
    const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t;
  }

  function heapSortSub(start, end) {
    const len = end - start;
    function heapify(i, heapSize) {
      let largest = i;
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let idx = i;
        if (l < heapSize && arr[start + l] > arr[start + idx]) idx = l;
        if (r < heapSize && arr[start + r] > arr[start + idx]) idx = r;
        if (idx !== i) { [arr[start + i], arr[start + idx]] = [arr[start + idx], arr[start + i]]; i = idx; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(len / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, len);
    for (let endIdx = len - 1; endIdx > 0; endIdx--) {
      swap(start, start + endIdx);
      heapify(0, endIdx);
    }
  }

  function quicksort3(start, end, depth) {
    if (end - start <= 1) return;
    if (depth <= 0) {
      heapSortSub(start, end);
      return;
    }
    const pivot = arr[start + Math.floor((start + end - 1) / 2)];
    let lt = start, i = start, gt = end - 1;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { swap(lt, i); lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { swap(i, gt); gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    quicksort3(start, lt, depth - 1);
    quicksort3(gt + 1, end, depth - 1);
  }

  quicksort3(0, n, depthLimit);
}
```

