## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates understanding of the Trie (prefix tree) data structure, covering insert, delete, and prefix-query operations with proper edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie node structure**: Each node stores 26 child pointers, an end-of-word flag, and a pass-through counter for efficient prefix queries
- **Safe deletion**: Decrement counts along the path and only unmark end-of-word when the word actually exists
- **Prefix query**: Return the count at the prefix's last node in O(length of prefix) time
- **Duplicate handling**: Adding the same word twice increments the counter twice; deleting decrements twice, effectively removing one occurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.isEndOfWord = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  add(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) {
        node.children[index] = new TrieNode();
      }
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count++;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = true;
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return;
      node = node.children[index];
      node.count--;
    }
    node.isEndOfWord = false;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const index = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.children[index]) return 0;
      node = node.children[index];
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Autocomplete for large vocabularies is common; a Trie yields fast prefix queries and ordered counts.

## Key Concepts
- Trie vs BST for prefix-based lookup
- DFS with pruning and max-heap to collect top-k results
- Memory optimizations: sparse maps, compact nodes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.word = null;
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word, delta) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) {
        cur.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }
    cur.word = word;
    cur.freq += delta;
  }

  search(word) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) return false;
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }
    return cur.word === word;
  }

  prefixTop(prefix, k) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) return [];
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (node) => {
      if (node.word) {
        results.push({ word: node.word, freq: node.freq });
        if (results.length > k) {
          results.sort((a, b) => b.freq - a.freq);
          results.pop();
        }
      }
      for (const child of node.next.values()) {
        dfs(child);
      }
    };

    dfs(cur);
    return results.sort((a, b) => b.freq - a.freq).slice(0, k).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

## Time/Space Trade-offs vs BST/AVL
- **Trie**: O(m) insert/search where m = word length, independent of vocabulary size. Prefix queries O(m + k) where k = results. Space O(α×m) where α = alphabet size.
- **BST/AVL**: O(log n) operations where n = vocabulary size. Prefix queries require O(n) traversal. Space O(n).
- **Trie advantage**: Constant-time prefix lookups, natural ordering by frequency, better cache locality for common prefixes.
- **BST advantage**: More memory efficient for sparse vocabularies, simpler implementation.

## Memory-heavy Vocabulary Justification
For large vocabularies, use:
- **Sparse maps** (Map/Object) instead of fixed arrays for children
- **Node compression** (radix/patricia tries) to merge common prefixes
- **Frequency-based pruning** to remove low-frequency words
- **External storage** for very large datasets, keeping hot prefixes in memory

Tries excel when prefix queries dominate and memory can be optimized through compression techniques.

